The purpose of this observation is to ensure that the returned value respects the PUT contract, as explained above.

To understand this generated test: 

The PUT contract states that the `IndexOf` operation should be a pure function under the assumption new addition of the input shouldn't affect the previous operation results. So, the old_arrList_Count == new_arrList_Count, old_arrList_x == old_x, and old_arrList_contains_x == new_arrList_contains_x, old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x <= new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x (This is because index of operation may increase the last occurrence of that value)  If so, the IndexOf would be a proper PUT operation and any additional assertions would pass. 

Remember to run your test with the Pex settings. This Pex test will increase the probability of detecting interesting test cases beyond the input values specified in the test method. It may generate unreachable code that the runtime does not consider.

Please keep this in mind: Pex is a tool to generate tests during the code review stage. It gives you an opportunity to share and receive good code reviews and helps in understanding the functionality more thoroughly. But it is based on human intuition and experiences which can lead to unexpected assertions or errors. Use it at your own discretion.--------------------            Assert.IsTrue(New_Ret < 0 || Old_arrListLastIndexOfX <= New_arrListCount);

