A: This type of test is called unit testing and it's one option of automated code testing. Unfortunately, it's not directly related with fuzz testing.
Unlike fuzz testing where the goal is not to break your program, but to reveal bugs, unit testing has an easier goal: to confirm that your program runs correctly, and produce the expected output for the inputs it's designed to process. 
Indeed, the kinds of conditions you list in contracts are the "preconditions" of methods (inputs to the method) and "postconditions" of methods--------------------            Assert.IsTrue(New_Ret <= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ If the return value if the last index of x in the old array is equal than the new array count then contains x 
            if(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX >= New_arrListCount)
            {
                Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListContainsX);
            }
            else
            {
                Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListContainsX == New_arrListContainsX);
            }
        }
    }
}--------------------            // @@@ If the return value is non-negative, x is in old array and in new array
            // @@@ If the return value is non-negative, x is not in old array but in new array
            // @@@ If the return value is negative, old array does not contain x but new array contains x

            AssumePostcondition.IsTrue(true);
        }
    }
